When a callback’s realm’s global object is a Window whose associated
Document isn’t fully active, Ladybird refuses to call it — whether
invoking or constructing a callback function, or calling a callback
interface’s operation — and throws a NotSupportedError. Gecko’s
CallSetup::GetActiveGlobalObjectForCall(), which all three go through,
does the same for a callback whose window fails HasActiveDocument(),
throwing a NotSupportedError (“Refusing to execute function from window
whose document is no longer active.”). Blink’s generated callback
bindings throw “The provided callback is no longer runnable.” when
IsCallbackFunctionRunnable() fails, i.e. once the callback’s context is
invalid or destroyed — and Blink checks the incumbent context too.

WebKit has a check of its own, but it doesn’t catch these callbacks. Its
generated callbacks return UnableToExecute when ActiveDOMCallback::
canInvokeCallback() fails, which happens only once the callback’s
ScriptExecutionContext has stopped or suspended its active DOM objects.
And Safari still calls a callback from a removed iframe, or from a
navigated-away document. WebKit also exempts a few callbacks from that
check with [SkipCallbackInvokeCheck], NodeFilter’s acceptNode() among
them; Ladybird, Gecko, and Blink refuse those like any other.
